    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The physical characteristics of an ecosystem provide the framework for its biological structure, and changes to this framework can have widespread ecological impacts.  While it is well documented that the kelp forest in Monterey Bay experiences frequent low oxygen (hypoxic) episodes, little is known about how these episodes affect kelp forest organisms.  To answer this question, specimens of Sebastes mystinus (blue rockfish) were collected from the wild and C-start escape responses—burst swimming to evade predation—were elicited in the laboratory using a dropped weight method. Trials were performed both in normoxic conditions and discrete low oxygen levels spanning levels measured in the field.  Each C-start escape was recorded at high speed with a digital video camera and analyzed in ImageJ to quantify swimming performance and responsiveness, both indicators of ability to escape predators and thus of fitness.  As oxygen levels declined, fewer individuals responded to the startle stimulus, indicating lower escape performance in hypoxia.  Significant changes to escape response duration and final escape velocity were also observed with changes in dissolved oxygen concentration, possibly representing strategies for mediating physiological stress in hypoxia and conserving energy.  Physiological stress could motivate S. mystinus and other kelp-associated species to avoid hypoxic areas, a distributional shift that could alter the structure of the kelp forest ecosystem and interactions among its associated species.  Exploring the impacts of hypoxia on kelp forest organisms is crucial to understanding how these ecosystems will change in response to changes in the physical environment.",
